AT="Not a valid term to make a name from",kAU="Not a valid expression to make a name from",kAP="{empty type pattern hole}",kAQ="{multi type pattern hole}",kAE="No typ info found",kAD="No tpat info found",kAC="No pat info found",kAB="No exp info found",kBk="Pat is not a pattern",kBj="Body of let/type alias/module is not an expression",kBi="Current node is not a let, type alias, or module expression",kBh="get_refs_to_after_pattern_edit: expected expression infos for both arguments",kA_="Current node is not an expression",kBa=[0,0],kBb=[0,0],kBc=[0,0],kBd=[0,0],kBe=[0,0],kBf=[0,0],kA$=da1,kBV=[1,10],kBJ=[1,10],kBK=[1,10],kBL="Failed trying to rename all occurences of the pattern. Could not find the old pattern in the statics map.",kBM="Failed trying to rename all occurences of the pattern. Could not find the new pattern in the statics map.",kBO=gw,kBP=gw,kBN=gw,kBQ=[1,10],kBR=[1,10],kBS=[1,[0,"Deleting a definition or pattern is not yet implemented."]],kBT=gw,kBU=gw,kBG=[1,0],kBH=[1,3],kBF=[1,3],kBD=[1,2],kBE=[1,3],kBC=m,kBB=[0,gI,[0,hy,[0,cb,0]]],kBz=m,kBA=m,kBy=[0,0,m],kBx=[0,bXs,1],kBw=[0,fi],kBv=gw,kBn=[0,1,1,0],kBo=[0,1,1,1],kBp=[0,1,0,0],kBq=[0,0,1,0],kBr=[0,0,0,1],kBt=[0,0,1,0],kBs=[0,0,0,0],kBu=[0,0,0,0],kBl="Current node is not a let or type alias expression, so no pattern to update",kBm=da1,kB0=[0,1,0,0,1,0,Py,0,0,1,1,1,0],kB1=[0,0],kBZ=[0,[0,c6,[0,ay,0]]],kBX=m,kCq=[0,0],kCo=[0,m],kCp=[0,m],kCr="Select not implemented for goals",kCs=[1,3],kCt=[1,3],kB9=c1,kB_=[0,[4,0,0,0,0],bf],kB$=ay,kCa=cx,kB2=[0,[18,[1,[0,[11,d,0],d]],[11,a6,0]],a8],kB3=s2,kB4=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,C,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],D],kB5=[0,[17,0,0],A],kB6=[0,[12,59,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]],E],kB7=ae1,kB8=[0,[18,[1,[0,0,m]],[2,0,[11,C,[17,[0,a,1,0],0]]]],D],kCb=[0,[17,0,0],A],kCc=[0,[17,[0,a,1,0],[12,aS,[17,0,0]]],a9],kCl=ae1,kCm=s2,kCj=ae1,kCk=s2,kCh=[0,ae1],kCi=[0,s2],kCf=ae1,kCg=s2,kCd=dC9,kCu=kr(["## Hazel language guide",m,"Hazel is a typed, live functional programming language with structure-aware editing.","Every edit state maintains a valid AST. Typed holes (`?`) represent missing code and still typecheck/evaluate.",m,"## Critical Syntax Rules",m,"1. **Function application** ALWAYS uses parentheses: `f(x)`, `g(x, y)`. NEVER use space-separated application.","2. **Function parameters** are comma-separated: `fun x, y -> expr`. NEVER space-separate parameters.","3. **Named functions** \xe2\x80\x94 PREFERRED: parameters on the let, return type after the parens: `let f(x, y) = expr in ...`, `let f(x: Int, y: Int): Int = expr in ...`. Equivalent to `let f = fun x, y -> expr in ...`; keep `fun` for anonymous lambdas.","4. **Recursion** needs NO `rec` keyword. `let f(n) = ... f(n - 1) ... in ...` works directly, annotated or not.","5. **Pattern matching** uses `case expr | p1 => e1 | p2 => e2 end`. MUST end with `end`. NO `of`, `with`, `switch`, or `match` keywords.","6. **Type aliases**: `type Name = definition in body`.","7. **Sum types** (ADTs): `+ Ctor1 + Ctor2(Type)`. Each constructor prefixed with `+`.","8. **List types**: `[Int]`, `[String]`.","9. **Tests**: `test bool_expr end`. MUST end with `end`. Chain with `;`.","10. **Comments**: `# text #`. MUST have BOTH opening AND closing `#`. Cannot span multiple lines.","11. **No dot-accessor** on plain tuples \xe2\x80\x94 use destructuring: `let (a, b) = tuple in ...`. Labeled-tuple/module fields DO use `.` (see Modules).","12. **FORBIDDEN keywords**: `rec`, `switch`, `match`, `with`, `of`. NEVER use them.","13. **Reserved words** \xe2\x80\x94 these lex as keyword forms and can NEVER be variable/function names (e.g. `eval` becomes the eval-filter keyword and misparses): `case`, `debug`, `eval`, `fix`, `forall`, `fun`, `hide`, `hint`, `if`, `in`, `let`, `module`, `pause`, `test`, `theorem`, `type`, `typfun`, `use` (also `proof_object` and the `of_*` prover forms). Common traps: `eval`, `test` \xe2\x80\x94 name things `evaluate`, `run_tests`, etc. `true`/`false`/`undefined`/`_` are literals/wildcard, equally unusable. `rec` and `poly` are reserved only in TYPE positions (recursive/polymorphic types \xe2\x80\x94 there is no let-rec). Proof-syntax delimiters (`val`, `valid`, `consistent`, `matched_*`) and trailing delimiters (`end`, `then`, `else`, `with`) DO parse as ordinary names, but avoid them for clarity.","14. **No modulo operator** `%`. Use the `int_mod(a, b)` builtin instead.","15. **Modules**: `{ let x = 1; let y = 2 }` \xe2\x80\x94 curly braces, semicolon-separated items. Use `module M = { ... }` for capitalized names.",m,"## Types",m,i$,"Int, Float, Bool, String                  # Primitives #","(Int, String)                             # Tuple #","[Int]                                     # List #","Int -> Bool                               # Function #","(Int, Int) -> Int                         # Multi-param function #","+ None + Some(Int)                        # Sum type #","poly A -> A -> A                          # Polymorphic #",i$,m,"## Operators",m,i$,"+, -, *, /, **     # Int arithmetic #","+., -., *., /., **.   # Float arithmetic (dotted ops required) #","==, !=, <, >, <=, >=  # Comparison (result is Bool) #","==., !=., <., >., <=., >=.  # Float comparison #","&&, ||, !          # Boolean logic #","++                 # String concatenation #","::                 # List cons (prepend) #","@                  # List append #","|>                 # Pipeline: x |> f is f(x) #",i$,m,"## Let Bindings",m,i$,"let x = 42 in","let y : Int = x + 1 in",'let greeting = "hello" in',hV,i$,m,"Bindings can be chained. The final expression is the program's result.",m,"## Functions",m,i$,"# Anonymous function #",dmC,m,"# Multi-parameter #","fun x, y -> x + y",m,"# Named function (preferred) #","let add(x, y) = x + y in",bRQ,m,"# With type annotations #","let add(x: Int, y: Int): Int = x + y in",bRQ,m,"# Equivalent explicit form #","let add : (Int, Int) -> Int = fun x, y -> x + y in",bRQ,m,"# Recursive (no rec keyword needed) #","let factorial(n) =","  if n <= 1 then 1 else n * factorial(n - 1)",fi,"factorial(5)",i$,m,"## Algebraic Data Types and Pattern Matching",m,i$,"# Define a sum type #","type Shape =","  + Circle(Int)","  + Rect(Int, Int)",fi,m,"# Pattern match with case...end #","let describe(s) =","  case s",'  | Circle(r) => "circle"','  | Rect(w, h) => "rectangle"',aMi,fi,"describe(Circle(5))",i$,m,"## Lists",m,i$,"let xs = [1, 2, 3] in","let ys = 0 :: xs in",m,"# Process with pattern matching #","let length(xs: [Int]): Int =",bMz,dKL,"  | _ :: tl => 1 + length(tl)",aMi,fi,"length(ys)",i$,m,"## Tuples",m,i$,"let point : (Int, Int) = (3, 4) in","let (x, y) = point in","x + y",i$,m,"## Conditionals",m,i$,'if x > 0 then "positive" else "non-positive"',i$,m,"## Polymorphism",m,i$,"# Type function with poly annotation #","let id : poly A -> A -> A =","  typfun A -> fun x : A -> x",fi,"id@<Int>(42)",m,"# Polymorphic map #","let map : poly A -> poly B -> ((A -> B), [A]) -> [B] =","  typfun A -> typfun B -> fun f, xs ->","    case xs","    | [] => []","    | hd :: tl => f(hd) :: map@<A>@<B>(f, tl)","    end",fi,"map@<Int>@<Bool>(fun n -> n > 1, [1, 2, 3])",i$,m,"## Mutual Recursion (via tuple binding)",m,i$,"let (even : Int -> Bool, odd : Int -> Bool) =","  (fun n -> if n == 0 then true else odd(n - 1),","   fun n -> if n == 0 then false else even(n - 1))",fi,"even(4)",i$,m,"## Modules",m,"Modules use curly braces `{ }` with semicolon-separated items. They are syntactic sugar for labeled tuples.",m,i$,"# Basic module #","let m = { let x = 1; let y = true } in m.x",m,"# With type alias (ascription on right) #","let m = { type T = Int; let x = 5 : T } in m.x",m,"# The `module` keyword for capitalized names #","module M = { let x = 1; let y = 2 } in M.x + M.y",m,"# Nested modules (module keyword inside body for capitalized names) #","let m = { module Inner = { let z = 42 }; let r = Inner.z } in m.r",m,"# Nested with lowercase let #","let outer = { let inner = { let a = 42 } } in outer.inner.a",m,"# Qualified type access #","module M = { type T = Int } in","let x : M.T = 6 in x",i$,m,"Each item is one of: `let pat = exp`, `type T = typ`, `module M = { ... }` (capitalized names), or a bare expression (e.g. `test ... end`).","Use `module` for capitalized names; lowercase `let` works for module bindings.",m,"## Tests",m,i$,"test 2 + 2 == 4 end;","test factorial(5) == 120 end;",i$,m,"## Typed Holes",m,"The `?` symbol is a typed hole \xe2\x80\x94 a placeholder for missing code.","Programs with holes still typecheck, evaluate, and provide meaningful feedback.","Use holes as scaffolding when building programs incrementally.",m,i$,"let f : ? -> ? = fun x -> x + 1 in",dEw,i$,m,"## Complete Example Program",m,"This program defines an Option type, a list utility, and tests:",i$,"type Option = + None + Some(Int) in",m,"let unwrap_or(opt: Option, default: Int): Int =","  case opt","  | None => default","  | Some(x) => x",aMi,fi,m,"let sum(xs: [Int]): Int =",bMz,dKL,"  | hd :: tl => hd + sum(tl)",aMi,fi,m,"let safe_head(xs: [Int]): Option =",bMz,"  | [] => None","  | hd :: _ => Some(hd)",aMi,fi,m,"test sum([1, 2, 3]) == 6 end;","test unwrap_or(safe_head([10, 20]), 0) == 10 end;","test unwrap_or(safe_head([]), 0) == 0 end;","sum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])",i$,m]),kCv='\nThe following demonstrates the ideal workflow pattern for implementing a user request.\nStudy this example carefully \xe2\x80\x94 it shows HOW to think, plan, and act.\n\n---\n\n### Example: User asks "Implement a fibonacci function and test it"\n\n**Starting program state:**\n```\n?\n```\n\n**Step 1 \xe2\x80\x94 Plan.** (Illustrates the workbench on a **multi-step feature**; skip this entirely for small/trivial edits.) Create a task with meaningful milestones (not micro-steps).\n\nTool call: `create_new_task`\n```json\n{\n  "task": {\n    "title": "Implement fibonacci with tests",\n    "description": "Implement a recursive fibonacci function and add test cases to verify correctness.",\n    "subtasks": [\n      {\n        "title": "Implement fibonacci function",\n        "description": "Define a recursive fib function: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1, fib(n)=fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)."\n      },\n      {\n        "title": "Add test cases",\n        "description": "Add tests for base cases and a few recursive cases."\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Step 2 \xe2\x80\x94 Implement.** The program is empty (`?`), so use `insert_before` (or `insert_after`) with **no** `path` to seed the first binding. In real tasks, prefer a **minimal** first insert and then `insert_*` / `update_*` for the rest; this walkthrough uses one larger insert only to keep the transcript short.\n\nTool call: `insert_before`\n```json\n{\n  "code": "let fib(n: Int): Int =\\n  if n <= 0\\n    then 0\\n    else if n == 1\\n      then 1\\n      else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\\nin"\n}\n```\n\nAgent sees the updated program (via context):\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  if n <= 0\n    then 0\n    else if n == 1\n      then 1\n      else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\nin\n?\n```\nNo static errors. Mark first subtask complete:\nTool call: `mark_active_subtask_complete`\n```json\n{ "summary": "Implemented recursive fibonacci: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1, fib(n)=fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)." }\n```\n\n**Step 3 \xe2\x80\x94 Add tests.** Update the body of `fib` (the `?` after `in`) with test expressions.\n\nTool call: `update_body`\n```json\n{\n  "path": "fib",\n  "code": "test fib(0) == 0 end;\\ntest fib(1) == 1 end;\\ntest fib(5) == 5 end;\\ntest fib(10) == 55 end;\\nfib(10)"\n}\n```\n\nAgent sees:\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  if n <= 0\n    then 0\n    else if n == 1\n      then 1\n      else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\nin\ntest fib(0) == 0 end;\ntest fib(1) == 1 end;\ntest fib(5) == 5 end;\ntest fib(10) == 55 end;\nfib(10)\n```\nNo static errors. All tests pass. Mark subtask and task complete:\nTool call: `mark_active_subtask_complete`\n```json\n{ "summary": "Added 4 test cases covering base cases (0, 1) and recursive cases (5, 10)." }\n```\nTool call: `mark_active_task_complete`\n```json\n{ "summary": "Implemented recursive fibonacci function with type annotation and 4 passing test cases." }\n```\n\n**Final message to user:** "The `fib` function is implemented and all 4 test cases pass. It handles base cases (0 and 1) and recursively computes fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2) for larger values."\n\n---\n\n### Example: User asks "Add a helper function that computes fib for a list of numbers"\n\n**Starting program state:**\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  \xe2\x8b\xb1\nin\ntest fib(0) == 0 end;\ntest fib(1) == 1 end;\ntest fib(5) == 5 end;\ntest fib(10) == 55 end;\nfib(10)\n```\n\nNote: `fib` is collapsed (\xe2\x8b\xb1). The agent needs to understand what `fib` does but doesn\'t need to see its implementation \xe2\x80\x94 the tests provide enough context.\n\n**Step 1 \xe2\x80\x94 Plan.**\n\nTool call: `create_new_task`\n```json\n{\n  "task": {\n    "title": "Add map_fib helper",\n    "description": "Add a function that maps fib over a list of integers, plus test cases.",\n    "subtasks": [\n      {\n        "title": "Implement map_fib",\n        "description": "Insert a map_fib function after fib that applies fib to each element of an Int list."\n      },\n      {\n        "title": "Update program output with tests",\n        "description": "Replace the current program body with tests for map_fib and a demo call."\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Step 2 \xe2\x80\x94 Insert the new function.** Use `insert_after` to add a binding after `fib`.\n\nTool call: `insert_after`\n```json\n{\n  "path": "fib",\n  "code": "let map_fib(ns: [Int]): [Int] =\\n  case ns\\n  | [] => []\\n  | hd :: tl => fib(hd) :: map_fib(tl)\\n  end\\nin"\n}\n```\n\n**Step 3 \xe2\x80\x94 Update the body** to include tests for the new function.\n\nTool call: `update_body`\n```json\n{\n  "path": "map_fib",\n  "code": "test map_fib([]) == [] end;\\ntest map_fib([0, 1, 5]) == [0, 1, 5] end;\\nmap_fib([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])"\n}\n```\n\nNo static errors.\n\n**Step 4 \xe2\x80\x94 Verify with probes.** Use probes to confirm the output is correct.\n\nTool call: `place_probe`\n```json\n{ "paths": ["map_fib"] }\n```\n\nAgent sees (with probe results):\n```\nlet fib(n: Int): Int =\n  \xe2\x8b\xb1\nin\nlet map_fib(ns: [Int]): [Int] =\n  case ns\n  | [] => []                                   \xe2\x89\xa1 []\n  | hd :: tl => fib(hd) :: map_fib(tl)        \xe2\x89\xa1 [1, 1, 2, 3, 5]\n  end\nin\ntest map_fib([]) == [] end;\ntest map_fib([0, 1, 5]) == [0, 1, 5] end;\nmap_fib([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])                      \xe2\x89\xa1 [1, 1, 2, 3, 5]\n```\n\nThe `\xe2\x89\xa1` values confirm `map_fib` works correctly. Clean up the probe and mark complete:\nTool call: `remove_probe` \xe2\x86\x92 `mark_active_subtask_complete` \xe2\x86\x92 `mark_active_task_complete`\n\n**Final message to user:** "Added `map_fib` which maps the fibonacci function over a list. Verified with probes and 2 test cases."\n\n---\n\n### Key Patterns to Notice\n\n1. **Plan when it helps**: For big, multi-turn work, use coarse workbench milestones; for quick edits, skip tasks and edit directly.\n2. **Use the right tool**: on an empty scratchpad, use `insert_after`/`insert_before` with **no** `path` to seed the first binding (keep it small when possible); path-based `insert_after`/`insert_before` for adding bindings, `update_definition`/`update_body` for modifying existing code \xe2\x80\x94 prefer **many small** edits over one huge paste.\n3. **Manage context**: Collapsed definitions (\xe2\x8b\xb1) don\'t need to be expanded if tests or context already explain what they do. Expand only what you need.\n4. **Verify with probes**: After implementing, use `place_probe` to observe runtime values and confirm correctness. Clean up with `remove_probe` when done.\n5. **Check static errors**: After each edit, check the context for type errors. Fix them before moving on.\n6. **Communicate clearly**: Tell the user what was done and why, without referencing tool internals.\n',kDd=[0,1],kCX=[0,[18,[1,[0,[11,d,0],d]],[11,a6,0]],a8],k